      "problem": "The angle $\\theta =-{530}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{530}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{530}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-170$\n\n- $-{530}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={190}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${190}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{530}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977500/assets/1e741939f538fc5dfba9b5ff6f3b4ce0e68c5ebd746dfae533d3209c0c29feba.png)\n\nWe see that ${190}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant III. Therefore, $-{530}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant.",
